Herein, a novel strategy for ultrafast transformation of MOF bulk into ultrathin metal oxyhydroxide nanosheets for efficient OER has been developed (Scheme 1). Two isomeric MOFs (FJ1-H25Fe and FJ1-H25FeCo) with different chemical stabilities are prepared through reaction of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) ligand with Fe clusters or Co-doped Fe clusters, respectively. Electric-field assisted hydrolysis of metastable FeCo-MOF (FJ1-H25FeCo) will immediately result to FeCo-oxyhydroxides (FeCo-MOF-EH) nanosheets with 3 nm thickness, while more stable Fe-MOF (FJ1-H25Fe) bulk only leads to block of hydrolysis products (FeMOF-EH) under the same treatments. Further researches demonstrate that the formation of nanosheets should result from following two key processes: I. Alkali induced hydrolysis of metastable FeCo-MOF bulk into block of hydrolysis products (FeCo-MOF-H), in which Co partial substitution makes FeCo-MOF can be hydrolyzed easily in the alkaline condition. II. Electric-field driven reconstitution of FeCo-MOF-H bulk into FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets, where the cooperative transfer of various charged molecules (OH anions, \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) molecules, charged Co complexes) inside the bulk will destroy the bulk along the direction of electric-field and the \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) ligand can be directly recovered and reused. The in-situ made FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets on Ni foam exhibit excellent OER performances, displaying an extremely low overpotential of \(231\;\;\mathsf{m V}\) at the current density of \(10\,\mathsf{m A\,c m}^{-2}\) , a relatively small Tafel slope of 42 \(\mathsf{m V\ c e c}^{-1}\) , a remarkably large \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d l}}\) value of \(105.8\;\mathsf{m F\;c m}^{-2}\) , and at least long-term durability of \(30\;\mathsf{h}\) . This work not only provides a novel strategy for ultrafast and facile preparation of low-cost and efficient electrocatalysts for OER, but also represents a new way for the preparation of metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets with good crystallinity and morphology and a fresh method for mild synthesis of nanosized derivatives from MOF materials.

