Synthesis of Fe-MOF and FeCo-MOF

In order to construct MOF with controllable hydrolysis rate, a highly hard metal cluster \(\left[\mathsf{F e}_{3}(\mu_{3}-0)(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{\mathsf{e}}\right]\) which also can be easily doped has been selected as secondary building units (SBUs)[13], and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) with different coordination atoms has

been selected as organic ligand.[14] The Fe-MOF (FJ1-H25Fe) \(\{\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{I I I})[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{I I I})]_{3}(\mu_{3}-0)(\mathsf{B T T A})_{2.5}(\mathsf{H C O O})_{2}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})(\mathsf{N M F})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}\}\) was obtained from a solvothermal reaction between metal clusters \(\left[\mathsf{F e}_{3}(\mu_{3}-0)(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{\mathsf{e}}\right]\) (Figure 1a) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) ligand (Figure 1b) in the N-methylformamide (NMF) solvent. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis indicates that FJ1-H25Fe crystallizes in the monoclinic system with the \(\mathsf{P}2_{1}/\mathsf{I}\) space group, which consists of two types of Fe-based SBUs and \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands. Each six-connected trimeric SBU (SBU I) is associated with two quadridentate \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands, three bidentate \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands, one bridging \(\mathsf{H C O O}^{-}\) group from the decomposition of NMF, and terminated by two \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) molecules and one vestigial \(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O}^{-}\) group. The three-connected mononuclear SBU (SBU II) is firstly coordinated with two triazolate N atoms from different quadridentate \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands and one carboxylate O atom of the bridging \(\mathsf{H C O O}^{-}\) group, then blocked by three O atoms from NMF, \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) , and isolated formic acid, finally resulting in an octahedral \([\mathsf{C O N}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}]\) coordination geometry (Figure 1c). In the framework of FJ1-H25Fe, each trinuclear cluster joins with a mononuclear node through bridged a formic acid and shared a quadridentate \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) linker (Figure S1). The \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands have two different linking modes, the quadridentate one serves as a four-connected node binding with two trinuclear clusters and two mononuclear nodes, and the bidentate one acts as a two-connected linker concatenating two trinuclear clusters (Figure 1d), finally leading to an extended 3D framework with 1D channels (Figure 1e). The total solvent accessible volume calculated by PLATON is \(40.9\%\) of the unit cell volume with a probe radius of \(1.8\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) .[15] The limiting pore diameter and the maximum pore diameter of FJ1-H25Fe are estimated to be \(4.3\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) and \(6.1\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) by \(Z\in\infty++\) software.[16] Interestingly, the channel walls are densely stacked with the uncoordinated triazolate N atoms and other polar coordinated molecules, such polar chemical environment should be beneficial for OH anions diffusing. From the topological point of view, the framework of FJ1-H25Fe can be simplified as a 3, 4, 6-connected network with the point symbol of \((3.4.5^{3},6)(3.4.5)(3.4^{2},5^{6},7^{2},8^{3})\) by using the metal SBUs and quadridentate ligands as the nodes (Figure 1f). Co-doped may reduce the water stability of Fe-MOF (FJ1-H25Fe) and enhance the potential OER activity through synergistic effects. [7], 10c, 17] Hence, the bimetallic \(\mathsf{F e C o-M O F}\) (FJ1-H25FeCo) sample also was prepared by the solvothermal reaction between \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) ligand and the preformed Co-doped metal clusters \(\left[\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}(\mu_{3}-0)(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{\mathsf{e}}\right]\) , and its structure and purity were proved by powder X-ray diffraction analyses and energy-dispersive X-ray analyses. As shown in Figure S2, the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of as-synthesized FeCo-MOF (FJ1-H25FeCo) match with that of the as-made Fe-MOF (FJ1-H25Fe) and simulated Fe-MOF (FJ1-H25Fe) well. Also, the energy-dispersive X-ray analyses (EDX) elemental mappings demonstrate the highly homogeneous distributions of C, N, O, Fe in Fe-MOF and C, N, O, Fe, Co in FeCo-MOF, respectively (Figure S3, S4).
