Transformation of Fe-MOF and FeCo-MOF into corresponding metal oxyhydroxides through electric-field assisted hydrolysis

Before the hydrolysis, we firstly tested the stability of the two MOFs at different pH. As expected, Fe-MOF and FeCo-MOF display different stabilities in the same alkaline solution. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analyses indicate that the framework of Fe-MOF is stable even in the \(\mathsf{p H}=12\) solution while FeCo-MOF will be rapidly hydrolyzed at \(\mathsf{p H}=10\) (Figure S5). Compared with Fe-MOF, the less stability of FeCo-MOF for alkaline results from Co partial substitution, in which the weaker Co-O interaction than Fe-O makes the Co substitution position easier to be attacked by OH anions. So we tried to directly hydrolyze MOF bulk in a solution with stronger alkaline (1 M KOH) to lead to metal hydroxide or metal oxyhydroxide with well nanostructure.[18] However, immersing both Fe-MOF and FeCo-MOF bulk into 1 M KOH for 1 day, only bulk of hydrolysis products (namely Fe-MOF-H and FeCo-MOF-H) were obtained separately, in which bulk morphology kept unchanged after hydrolysis (Figure S6). In the process of hydrolysis, both free \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) molecules and free charged metal complexes should be in-situ produced. If an external electric-field is exerted, such new-formed free charged molecules will orderly transfer along the direction of electric-field, which may be beneficial for destruction of the MOF block into multiple nanostructures. Therefore, the electric-field assisted hydrolysis was carried out as following. The commercial carbon fiber paper (CFP) was

used as the support and current-collector. The ink of MOF precursors was firstly dropped on the CFP substrate serving as the working electrode, and then treated with ten cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements (about 600 s) from 1.2 to \(1.8\,\lor\) (vs. RHE, the same hereinafter) at the scan rate of \(20\;\mathrm{mv}\;\;\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) in 1 M KOH.

Electric-field assisted hydrolysis was monitored by field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM). As shown in SEM images, both the Fe-MOF and FeCo-MOF precursors displayed as microparticles dispersed randomly on CFP before the electric-field driven hydrolysis (Figure S7). For FeCo-MOF, after ten cyclic voltammetry measurements, the disorderly particles of FeCo-MOF transformed into large amounts of uniformly distributed nanoscale clusters (namely FeCo-MOF-EH), arraying regularly along the axial direction of the carbon fiber paper (Figure 2a-b). These new-formed nanoclusters were actually constituted by numerous wrinkled nanosheets with the thickness of approximately 3 nm (Figure 2c-d). The nanosheet morphology of FeCo-MOF-EH was further verified by transmission electronic microscopy (TEM) (Figure 2e). However, no similar morphological evolution could be observed from FeMOF under the same treatment, the as-prepared hydrolysis products (namely Fe-MOF-EH) still kept the blocky morphology (Figure S8).

In order to determine the compositions of FeCo-MOF-EH and Fe-MOF-EH, high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) was firstly used to record both new-formed samples. As for FeCo-MOF-EH, the lattice fringes with spacings of \(0.33\ \mathsf{nm}\), \(0.24\ \mathsf{nm}\) and \(0.17\ \mathsf{nm}\) may result from the (310) facet of FeOOH, (021) facet of CoOOH and (102) facet of \((\mathsf{F e}_{0.67}\mathsf{C o}_{0.33})\mathsf{O O H}\), respectively (Figure 2f). The well-defined lattice fringes indicate that the FeCo-MOF-EH is a kind of mixed-metal oxyhydroxides, which are composed of FeOOH, CoOOH, \((\mathsf{F e}_{0.67}\mathsf{C o}_{0.33})\mathsf{O O H}\). Such transformation also can be further proved by PXRD analyses. As displayed in Figure 3a, the initial diffraction patterns of FeCo-MOF disappear completely after hydrolysis, and the as-recorded X-Ray diffraction signals match well with the standard signals from FeOOH, CoOOH, and \((\mathsf{F e}_{0.67}\mathsf{C o}_{0.33})\mathsf{O O H}\). Both HR-TEM and PXRD analyses confirm that electric-field assisted hydrolysis can in-situ transform FeCo-MOF bulk into mixed-metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets with a thickness of \(3\ \mathsf{nm}\). For Fe-MOF-EH, the interplanar distances are determined to be \(0.33\ \mathsf{nm}\), \(0.2\ \mathsf{nm}\) and \(0.17\ \mathsf{nm}\), which should originate from the (110), (120), and (211) plane of FeOOH, respectively (Figure S8d). PXRD analyses further confirm this conversion. As shown in Figure S9, the initial diffraction signals from Fe-MOF entirely disappear after hydrolysis, and the strong X-Ray diffraction signal attributed to (110) crystal facet of FeOOH has been observed, which proves that electric-field assisted hydrolysis also can transform Fe-MOF into FeOOH. However, the similar blocky morphology of Fe-MOF-EH as Fe-MOF implies that such conversion may only occur on the surface of Fe-MOF bulk.

In order to further get insight into the evolution from MOF precursors to metal oxyhydroxides, the surface chemical composition and valence state were also determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The XPS survey spectra confirm that the elemental components do not change during the electric-field assisted hydrolysis (Figure 3b, S11a), but their contents vary a lot (Table S4-S6). As for the C 1s and N 1s spectra, the peaks attributed to the carboxylate and triazolate functional groups completely disappear after electric-field
assisted hydrolysis, implying the dissociation of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{A}\) ligand from the surfaces of both Fe-MOF (Figure S11c, e) and FeCo-MOF (Figure S10a, c). The O 1s peaks of pristine Fe-MOF (Figure S11d) and FeCo-MOF (Figure S10b) result from M-O, \(\scriptstyle0=\complement-0\) , and \(-0\mathsf{H}\) of the \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{A}\) ligand, separately; however, only two type of peaks attributed to \(\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{O}\) and \(\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{O}\,\mathsf{H}\) have been observed in the O 1s spectra of Fe-MOF-EH and FeCo-MOF-EH. The clearly occurrence of \(\mathsf{M}{-}\mathsf{O}\,\mathsf{H}\) signals confirms the formation of metal oxyhydroxides. Furthermore, the oxidation state and electronic environment of metal sites also display noticeable variation after electric-field assisted hydrolysis. Compared with Fe-MOF, the Fe \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}\) peak in the XPS spectrum of Fe-MOF-EH positively shifts about \(0.4~{\mathsf{e V}}\) (Figure S11b), and the ratio of \(\mathsf{F e}^{3+}/\mathsf{F e}^{2+}\) increases almost 3.5 times (Table S4). This tendency suggests that the average valence state of Fe species in FeMOF-EH is in a high oxidation state, which also means the formation of FeOOH. Analogously, the Fe \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}\) peak of FeCoMOF-EH also positively shifts about \(0.4\,\mathsf{e V}\) (Figure 3c), and the ratio of \(\mathsf{F e}^{3+}/\mathsf{F e}^{2+}\) increases about 2.2 times, as well (Table S5). Notably, the Co2p spectra of FeCo-MOF-EH can be fitted into three pairs of peaks, the peaks at 797.8 eV and 781.7 eV belongs to the \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{2+}\) species and those at \(795.8\,{\sf e V}\) and \(780.9\,{\sf e V}\) corresponds to the \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{3+}\) species, while the last one pair of peaks are their satellites (Figure 3d). The appearance of \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{3+}\) signals in FeCo-MOF-EH is a sharp contrast with FeCo-MOF, which further proves the formation of the target metal oxyhydroxides. It is worthy to note that the Co \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}\) peak of FeCo-MOF-EH negatively shifts about \(0.9\,{\sf e V}\) compared with FeCo-MOF, which indicates that the synergistic effects between two metals in the as-made hybrid metal oxyhydroxides.[19] The dissociation of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{A}\) ligand and the formation of such metal oxyhydroxides could be further validated by Raman spectroscopy (Figure S10d, S11f) and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra (Figure S12).[20]

