As mentioned above, a new phenomenon that ultrafast transformation of Co-doped FeCo-MOF bulk into ultrathin mixed-metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets has been found. As a contrast, electric-field assisted hydrolysis of isomeric Fe-MOF bulk cannot result to similar nanostructure. Such two completely different hydrolysis behaviours prompt us to explore their potential mechanisms. Considering that the only difference between these two MOFs is Co-doped, this unusual phenomenon should result from partial substitution of Co. As mentioned above, after Co doping, the chemical stability of FeCo-MOF significantly reduce, which will be rapidly hydrolyzed in a \(\mathsf{p H}=10\) alkaline solution. Stronger alkaline (1 M KOH) should greatly accelerate the hydrolysis. Meanwhile, FeCo-MOF has polar channels, which may makes OH anions can diffuse into its pores and attack its framework from inside. In the process of rapid hydrolysis, a large number of free charged molecules including free \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligand and potential Co complexes may be in-situ produced on the surface and pores of FeCo-MOF. The existence of external electric-field may make the OH anions diffuse into the pores of FeCo-MOF more orderly and quickly, and then more free charged molecules will be produced. These new-formed free charged molecules also tend to transfer along the direction of electric-field. Such two electric-field driven synergistic transfers may lead to the breakdown of FeCo-MOF bulk from inside to outside. On the contrary, the isomeric FeMOF has a more stable framework; diffusion of the same amount of OH anions into its pores cannot effectively hydrolyze its framework from inside, so that the block shape of Fe-MOF can be retained after the same treatment. This can be confirmed by following three important experimental evidences. I. The new-formed nanoclusters are orderly distributed on the carbon fiber along the direction of electric-field rather than irregular distribution. This means that the deconstruction of MOF block is assisted by the electric-field. II. XPS results show that the content of cobalt in the new-formed metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets is significantly lower than that of FeCo-MOF precursors (Table S6). In addition, the inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analyses demonstrate that the electrolyte contains more cobalt after hydrolysis (Table S7).
These results indicate that the cobalt nodes are easier to be attacked by \(\mathsf{O H}^{-}\) anions and then convert into soluble free charged molecules. III. After electric-field assisted hydrolysis, \(76\%\) of \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands can be recovered. This demonstrates most of \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligands comprised in FeCo-MOF are dissociated. All of these evidences confirm that the deconstruction of FeCoMOF is caused by the synergistic transfer of \(\mathsf{O H}^{-}\) anions and new-formed free charged molecules along the direction of electric-field.

If so, above bulk of hydrolysis product (FeCo-MOF-H) also can transform into metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets after exerting an external electric-field. This is due to FeCo-MOF-H bulk already contains a large number of \(\mathsf{O H}^{-}\) anions and new-formed free charged molecules. So an external electric-field has been exerted on the FeCo-MOF-H bulk directly. As shown in Fig. S13a and S13b, when one single CV cycle (about 60 s) from 1.2 V to \(1.8~\lor\) was applied on the FeCo-MOF-H bulk samples, amounts of nanoclusters composed of nanosheets emerged and distributed along the direction of electric-field as we expected. In addition, to elucidate whether the emergence of nanosheets was a result of the electrochemical OER process, the CV cycle (about 20 s) within non-Faradaic potential range from \(1.2~\lor\) to \(1.4~\lor\) was conducted on the FeCo-MOF-H, and analogous nanosheets also could be observed (Figure S13c, d). Such phenomenon indicates that the morphologic evolution is induced by electric-field stimulation, rather than the redox process of OER reaction as previously reported.[13a, 25] Inspired by this, we finally find the FeCo-oxyhydroxides (FeCo-MOF-EH) nanosheets can be immediately prepared from direct hydrolysis of FeCo-MOF (FE:H25FeCo) bulk in the 1 M KOH solution assisted by one CV cycle (about 20 s) within non-Faradaic potential range from \(1.2\,\lor\) to \(1.4\,\lor\) (Figure S14).

So the possible evolution process from FeCo-MOF bulk to FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets based on the aforementioned evidences has been presented in detail as following. I. When the electrode containing FeCo-MOF bulk is immersed in alkaline solution, \(\mathsf{O H}^{-}\) anions begin to attack the surface and inside of FeCo-MOF bulk. II. Part of the framework inside the FeCo-MOF block is destroyed, and a large number of free charged molecules are formed at the same time. III. The synergistic transfer of \(\mathsf{O H}^{-}\) anions and new-formed free charged molecules deconstructs FeCo-MOF bulk into nanoclusters consisted of nanosheets. Meanwhile, the free \(\mathsf{B T T A}^{2}\) ligand and charged Co complexes will immediately escape from the surface of the new-formed nanosheets, leading to mixed-metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets with good crystallinity and morphology.

