# OER Properties

The electrocatalytic performance of the as-made samples was assessed with a typical three-electrode system in \(1.0\textsf{M}\) KOH aqueous solution at room temperature, and all of the catalysts were loaded onto the CFP. As a contrast, commercial \(\mathsf{I r O}_{2}\) benchmark was also measured under the same conditions. First, their polarization curves were gained by employing linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) at a scan rate of \(2\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) from 1.2 to \(1.8~\lor\) (Figure 4a). Remarkably, the results indicate that FeCo-MOF-EH possesses an outstanding activity with an relatively low overpotential of only \(302\;\mathsf{m V}\) to achieve the current density of 10 mA \(\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) , which is superior to that of Fe-MOF-EH \((381~\mathsf{m V})\) , FeCo-MOF-H \((353\;\mathsf{m V})\) , Fe-MOF-H \((512\;\mathsf{m V})\) , and even that of the benchmark \(\mathsf{I r O}_{2}\) \((342\,\,\mathsf{m V})\) . In order to get insight into the OER kinetics of the catalysts, their Tafel plots derived from the corresponding LSV curves were assessed (Figure 4b). Notably, the FeCo-MOF-EH exhibits a small Tafel slope of \(42\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}\) , much lower than that of Fe-MOF-EH \((50\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\) , FeCo-MOF-H \((51\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\) , Fe-MOF-H \((76\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\) and \(\mathsf{I r O}_{2}\) \((56\;\mathsf{m V}\;\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\) , demonstrating the faster reaction kinetics and favoured OER activity of FeCo-MOF-EH over the other four catalysts. In general, the superior OER electrocatalytic activity relates with the large electrochemical active surface area (ECSA). To further evaluate the ECSAs of catalysts, the double layer capacitance \((\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d l}})\) , which is positively proportional to ECSA, was calculated from the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves at various scan rates within the non-Faradaic region (Figure S15). Impressively, FeCo-MOF-EH possesses the highest \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d l}}\) value of \(81.5\;\mathsf{m F}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) (Figure 4c), which is almost nine-fold higher than that of FeMOF-EH \((9.6\;\mathsf{m F}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2})\) and Fe-MOF-H \((8.8\;\mathsf{m F}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2})\) and nearly two-fold higher than that of FeCo-MOF-H \((36.9\;\mathsf{m F}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2})\) . The striking contrast demonstrates that FeCo-MOF-EH has the largest active surface area and the most exposed active sites for OER. In addition to ECSA, the turnover frequencies (TOF) value also can reflect the intrinsic activity of catalysts. Assuming that all metal ions in the catalysts are OER active, the TOF value at the overpotential of \(300\,\mathrm{\mV}\) were calculated as \(0.062\ s^{-1}\) for FeCo-MOF-EH, which is prominently higher than that of FeMOF-EH \((0.0032\ s^{-1})\) , FeCo-MOF-H \((0.021\ s^{-1})\) and Fe-MOF-H \((0.00017\ s^{-1})\) , further validating the extraordinary OER activity of FeCo-MOF-EH. Moreover, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) also was carried out to further explore the transport resistance of the electrodes for better understanding the OER kinetics. Surprisingly, the FeCo-MOF-EH presents an extremely small semicircle in the Nyquist plots compared with that of Fe-MOF-EH, FeCo-MOF-H and Fe-MOF-H (Figure 4d). The charge transfer resistance \((\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}})\) value was calculated as 7.1 \(\Omega\) for FeCo-MOF-EH, strikingly about two orders of magnitude lower than that of Fe-MOF-EH \((179.4\ \Omega)\) , even three orders of magnitude lower than that of Fe-MOF-H \((1087.64\ \Omega)\) and four times lower than that of FeCo-MOF-H \((28.29\ \Omega)\) . Such a small charge transfer resistance implies that the charge transportation is much faster at the interface between FeCo-MOF-EH catalyst and the electrolyte during OER.[22]

Besides the excellent activities, long-term stability is also critically important for a potential electrocatalyst for practical applications. When estimated by galvanostatic measurement at a constant current of \(10\;\mathsf{m A}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) , FeCo-MOF-EH could remain nearly no decay within \(30\;\mathrm{~h~}\) , while the overpotential of commercial \(\mathsf{I r O}_{2}\) would increase \(20\%\) after \(25~\mathsf{h}\) (Figure 4e). In
addition, the excellent durability of FeCo-MOF-EH is further verified by the LSV curves which almost overlap before and after the galvanostatic test (Figure S16).

