The sharp contrast in OER activities between FeCo-MOF-EH and other three catalysts inspire us figure out the potential origination. It has been widely evidenced that the miniaturization in size and reduction in dimension could significantly promote the activity of catalysts.[71, 9a, 23] Herein, the formation of ultrathin nanosheets not only can significantly increase the amounts of accessible active sites for electrocatalysis, but also highly improve mass and charge transportation performance. In addition, the fine alignment of nanoclusters on CFP will augment the contact interface between the catalyst and the conductive support, and thereby accelerates the charge transfer rate, leading to the relatively small \(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}}\) value. Moreover, both the higher valence state of metal ions and hybrid metal contents will improve OER activity.[4a, 10f] In summary, all of these features comprised in FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets will synergistically promote its OER performances.

High conductivity is a crucial factor of a good catalyst. It should be noted that electrocatalysts integrated with conducting materials like Ni foam or Cu foam would display better electrocatalytic performance than their bulks, due to the higher conductivity, faster carrier transportation, and other synergistic

effect.[7m] In this context, we prepared FeCo-MOF-EH/NF by drop-casting the FeCo-MOF ink onto the Ni foam and then hydrolysing it in the alkaline solution assisted by electric-field. Notably, a large number of nanoclusters constituted of ultrathin nanosheets were also observed in the in-situ evolved FeCo-MOF-EH on Ni Foam (Figure S17). As we expected, FeCo-MOF-EH/NF not only exhibits an extremely low overpotential of \(231~\mathrm{mV}\) at the current density of \(10\;\mathsf{m A}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) (Figure 4f) but also has a relatively low Tafel slope of \(42\;\mathrm{mV}\) dec-1 (Figure S18a). Notably, the \(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}}\) value of FeCo-MOF-EH/NF dwindles to \(0.928\;\Omega\) owing to the increased conductivity of the substrate (Figure S18b). Besides, FeCo-MOF-EH/NF possesses remarkably large \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d t}}\) value of \(105.8~\mathsf{m F}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) (Figure S18d) and relatively high TOF value of \(0.35\ s^{-1}\) . The FeCo-MOF-EH/NF also performs strong durability up to \(30\;\mathsf{h}\) in the galvanostatic measurement at a constant current of \(10\;\mathsf{m A}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) (Figure S18e, f). All of these confirm that our FeCo-MOF-EH/NF is one of the few known high performing OER electrocatalysts (Table S8), exceeding most of the reported electrocatalysts based on metal oxyhydroxides nanosheets (Table S9).

It is worthy to note that the \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) ligand can be recovered and reused after electric-field assisted hydrolysis. The ligand dissociated in the alkaline electrolyte can be recovered by acidification and filtration, and the recovery efficiency was about \(76\%\) . The high purity of recovered ligand was confirmed by the \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR (Figure S19), which can be directly used to prepare FeCo MOF (FJ1-H25FeCo) samples without further purification. The recovery of ligand means that only cheap iron and cobalt salts are needed for the synthesis of FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets, which greatly reduces its cost.

In a word, the synthesis of FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets only requires consuming cheap iron salts and cobalt salts, due to the used \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T T A}\) ligand can be recovered and reused effectively. Meanwhile, the synthesis process is very mild, fast and efficient, without producing other impurities, and the in-situ synthesized nanosheets can be directly used for electrocatalysis without any post-treatment. Furthermore, the new-formed FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets have excellent OER properties. All of these confirm that the as-prepared FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets should be a kind of promising OER catalyst for industrial application.

# Conclusion

In summary, a novel strategy for ultrafast transformation of metal-organic framework (MOF) bulk into ultrathin metal oxyhydroxide (MOOH) nanosheets for efficient OER has been developed. Here, two isomeric MOFs with different chemical stability have been prepared through rational Co-doped, and only the metastable FeCo-MOF can be in-situ transformed into mixed-metal oxyhydrolyoxides nanosheets through electric-field assisted hydrolysis. The potential evolution process from FeCo-MOF bulk to FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets has been investigated in detail. The new-formed nanosheets exhibit excellent OER performance, displaying an extremely low overpotential of \(231~\mathrm{mV}\) at the current density of \(10\;\mathsf{m A}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) , a comparatively small Tafel slope of \(42\;\mathrm{mV}\) dec-1, a remarkably large \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{d t}}\) value of \(105.8~\mathsf{m F}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) and relatively high TOF value of \(0.35\;\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) . In a word, the new-formed FeCo-MOF-EH nanosheets not only have excellent OER properties, but also can be prepared in a mild, rapid, efficient and cheap way. This work not only provides a novel
